The exercise plots each product's sparkline on its own y-axis by setting same_limit to FALSE, so a product selling a thousand units and one selling five thousand reach the same peak height and read as equal. This alternative sets same_limit to TRUE so every sparkline shares one scale, and formats the Min, Max and Average columns to whole units. Putting all rows on a common scale makes it immediately clear which products genuinely sell more, because the height of each line now maps to real magnitude across the table.

### Extra Alternative 4: Colour-encoding target attainment


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
combined <- sales %>%
  left_join(spark, by = "Product") %>%
  left_join(bullet, by = "Product") %>%
  mutate(Attainment = Actual / Target) %>%
  arrange(desc(Attainment))

combined %>%
  gt() %>%
  gt_plt_sparkline('Monthly Sales') %>%
  gt_plt_bullet(column = Actual, target = Target, width = 28,
                palette = c("lightblue", "black")) %>%
  fmt_percent(columns = Attainment, decimals = 0) %>%
  fmt_number(columns = c(Min, Max, Average), decimals = 0) %>%
  data_color(columns = Attainment,
             fn = scales::col_bin(
               palette = c("#f4cccc", "#d9ead3"),
               domain = NULL,
               bins = c(0, 1, 5))) %>%
  gt_theme_538()
```

