The exercise pairs the bullet and sparkline but leaves target attainment for the reader to infer from the bar sitting against the red tick, with no flag for products that missed. This alternative adds an attainment column, sorts by it, and fills it red below target and green at or above using a two-bin colour cut. Carrying the exception in colour makes it immediately clear at a glance which products missed budget, before the reader parses any bar or number.

### Extra Alternative 5: Sortable data-bar magnitude column


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
report_bars <- report %>%
  mutate(Total = map_dbl(`Monthly Sales`, sum)) %>%
  select(Product, Total, `Monthly Sales`)

reactable(
  report_bars,
  defaultPageSize = 13,
  defaultSorted = "Total",
  defaultSortOrder = "desc",
  columns = list(
    Product = colDef(maxWidth = 200),
    Total = colDef(
      cell = data_bars(report_bars,
                       fill_color = "#1f77b4",
                       text_position = "outside-end",
                       number_fmt = scales::comma)),
    `Monthly Sales` = colDef(
      cell = react_sparkline(
        report_bars,
        highlight_points = highlight_points(min = "red", max = "blue")))
  )
)
```

